            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.069,
            0.488,
            0.086
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.886,
            0.262
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "was a profound state that was the goal of yoga and meditation. The word śhūnya means zero, and the word "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "śhūnyatā",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (zeroness) was used extensively in these works to describe the state that one is trying to reach during meditation — that of emptying one’s mind of all vṛttis (fluctuations of the mind) to achieve the state of perfect stillness and tranquility. Patanjali in the Yoga Sutras around the 3rd century BCE also describes how śhūnyatā can help lead to control over one’s mind, body, and senses."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.262,
            0.886,
            0.378
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Because Indian thinkers revered this state of ‘emptiness’, they possessed the conceptual framework necessary to welcome ‘nothingness’ as a concept in philosophy. This concept of zeroness then found its way into many fields, such as architecture, literature, linguistics, and eventually made its way into mathematics in the works of Āryabhaṭa and finally Brahmagupta."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.378,
            0.885,
            0.417
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Thus, the philosophical concept of emptiness crystallised into the mathematical zero."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.432,
            0.841,
            0.454
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.2.2 The Bakhśhālī Manuscript and Brahmagupta’s Rules"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.461,
            0.886,
            0.539
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In the Hindu Number System that we use today, the physical transition from a blank space to a symbol can be seen in the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Bakhśhālī Manuscript",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Dated to the early centuries CE, it features a bold dot (bindu) used to represent zero."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.539,
            0.886,
            0.635
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "However, a symbol is just a mark on a page until it has rules. The transformation of zero into a fully operational number occurred in the work of "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Brahmagupta",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". In his seminal work, the Brāhmasphuṭasiddhānta (628 CE), he explicitly defined zero as the result of subtracting a number from itself (a – a = 0)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.635,
            0.884,
            0.675
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Brahmagupta then laid down the fundamental laws of arithmetic with śhūnya:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.697,
            0.573,
            0.721
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Brahmagupta’s Rules for Zero"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.734,
            0.853,
            0.772
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When zero is added to a number, the number remains unchanged: "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a + 0 \\equiv a."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.776,
            0.854,
            0.814
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "• When zero is subtracted from a number, the number remains unchanged: "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a - 0 = a"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.819,
            0.83,
            0.838
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "• When any number is multiplied by zero, the result is zero: "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a \\times 0 = 0"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
